 ## 2026-08-27
 
+### 0732 — Extended lifecycle converges to a bounded HEV/runtime high-water (TASK-260715-135rr8)
+- INVESTIGATION: A bounded process-local 500-cycle SwiftPM/loopback run captured every physical-footprint jump and 100-cycle segment. Jumps ended at cycle 187; segments 201–300, 301–400, and 401–500 each changed by `0 B`. After the fixed 10-sample warmup, net growth was `180,248 B`; descriptors stayed `3`, threads stayed `8`, and every post-cleanup HEV session, read, and queue count was zero. Boundary start/stop and HEV start/main-return counters were exactly one per cycle.
+- RULE: The production evidence gate now requires at least 500 samples, the existing fixed 10-sample allocator warmup, a 250-sample trailing half-run with no footprint increase, and total post-warmup growth no greater than `256 KiB` (1% of the upper 25 MiB provisional envelope). Raw evidence records every parameter, transition count, jump cycle, ownership counter, and sample. Positive high-water convergence plus late-rise and oversized-high-water negative tests bind the classifier; the original rise-plus-plateau negative remains.
+- ANOMALY/FIX: The first extended attempt stopped at cycle 257 with Swift signal 5 because the test payload used checked `UInt8(iteration)`. `UInt8(truncatingIfNeeded:)` removes that artificial 255-cycle harness limit while address and port continue to identify each flow. The rerun passed 500 cycles in 20.91 seconds. No VPN, NetworkExtension, route, DNS, interface, packet-filter, SSH, or global-memory-pressure operation ran.
+
 ### 0716 — Correct lifecycle trend gate stops rev3 matrix (TASK-260715-135rr8)
 - FIX: The physical-matrix entry now independently computes the actual working candidate tree with a task-local temporary Git index and rejects a well-formed forged `RELUX_CANDIDATE_TREE_OID` before HEV work or evidence emission. It independently hashes the linked macOS HEV archive against the pinned manifest. Negative production-call-site coverage rejects forged tree and artifact hashes.
 - RULE: Lifecycle analysis uses an explicit fixed 10-sample allocator warmup. Across the remaining 90 samples it classifies any non-decreasing sequence with at least one increase as monotonic growth and records warmup/window/net-change plus increase/equal/decrease transition counts. Pure negative coverage flags rise-plus-plateau and accepts a plateau stabilized after warmup.
